    <Accessibility>
        <p>Notes for the two progress controls in the third example.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>ChunkProgressBarControl</code> renders <code>role="progressbar"</code> with <code>aria-valuemin</code>, <code>aria-valuemax</code> and <code>aria-valuenow</code> taken from <code>Min</code>, <code>Max</code> and the clamped <code>Value</code> (here a line count out of 5,000), and <code>aria-valuetext</code> with the whole-number percentage. While <code>Indeterminate</code> is true it leaves out <code>aria-valuenow</code> and the value text is "In progress". The segments are <code>aria-hidden</code>.</li>
            <li>Its accessible name is <code>AriaLabel</code>, "Progress" unless set. An <code>aria-label</code> attribute does not reach the element: the control captures unmatched attributes but never renders them.</li>
            <li><code>ProgressBarControl</code> renders no ARIA: no role, no value attributes and no live region. Its title, status, detail and error text are plain text, so a screen reader is not told it is a progress bar or when the text changes. In this example the chunk bars carry the progressbar role for the same count.</li>
            <li>The Cancel button is a native <code>button</code>, so it is in the tab order and responds to Enter and Space. Its <code>title</code> stays "Cancel" whatever <code>CancelText</code> says. With <code>CancelDisabled</code> it is disabled and drops out of the tab order.</li>
            <li>Motion: the chunk segments stop blinking under <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> and show solid blue instead. <code>ProgressBarControl</code>'s indeterminate sweep has no such rule and keeps moving.</li>
        </ul>
    </Accessibility>

